{{!
	Notion skin: the link primitive.

	Renders one anchor of skin chrome - a main-menu entry, a namespace or action tab, a
	dropdown row, a footer place, a user-menu item. It is the plain half of a menu item:
	MenuListItem.mustache renders Button.mustache from the positive data-button section
	and this partial from the inverted one, so a record carrying button styling never
	arrives here. The only partial it includes in turn is Icon.mustache.

	@typedef object attribute
	@prop string key the attribute name, for example href, class, title, accesskey, rel,
		id, or any aria- or data- name core chose to emit for this link
	@prop string value the attribute value, which arrives UNESCAPED from core and is
		therefore escaped here with a double-brace expression

	attribute[] array-attributes every attribute of the anchor, expanded one record at a
		time. Both href and class arrive this way, which is exactly why neither is written
		literally below: core assembled the class list before the data reached the skin,
		and a repeated class attribute would be discarded by the HTML parser rather than
		merged. An empty list is normal and yields a bare anchor.
	string|null icon optional Codex icon name for the leading glyph. A falsy value renders
		nothing at all - that is how a label-only link is expressed - and a truthy one is
		handed straight to Icon.mustache, which reads the name as the current context and
		so needs no key of its own.
	string text the link label, which also arrives unescaped from core and is escaped
		below.

	Where this data comes from, which is worth reading before renaming any key. These are
	core's own portlet link records. MediaWiki\Skin\Components\SkinComponentListItem
	assembles each list item's array-links by constructing a
	MediaWiki\Skin\Components\SkinComponentLink per link and collecting its template data;
	the records reach the skin as data-portlets.*.array-items[].array-links[]. (Do not
	expect to call SkinComponentLink::makeLink() yourself - it is private to that class.)
	That record shape - array-attributes plus text and icon - is the skin's ONE canonical
	link shape, and this partial is written against it, exactly as
	mediawiki-skins-Vector/includes/templates/Link.mustache is.

	Two producers speak that shape and both may be rendered here. NotionComponentMenu passes
	each core record through untouched apart from overriding icon and, where a menu asks for
	button styling, swapping the whole record for a data-button one. NotionComponentLink
	builds the links the skin invents rather than receives, and emits the same three keys:
	its attributes are assembled into array-attributes records rather than a pre-serialised
	attribute string, which is what lets either producer be rendered here without losing an
	attribute on the way in - the reason the two shapes were unified rather than left to be
	reconciled at each call site, since the attribute most easily lost is href.

	NotionComponentLink nevertheless has no call site in today's rendering path: no template
	includes >Link yet, because MenuListItem.mustache and MenuContents.mustache are later
	deliverables, and the class is currently exercised only through NotionComponentMenuListItem
	and its unit tests. It is kept faithful to the reference skin's component set for when
	those partials land, and unifying its shape with core's is what makes that landing a
	no-op rather than a repair.


	DO NOT REMOVE data-mw-interface, and do not make it conditional. Core's own client
	modules test for its PRESENCE, not its value, to decide that an element is trusted skin
	chrome rather than user-generated content. The selectors differ in where they expect the
	attribute, which is why it is emitted on the anchor here and why none of them may be
	assumed to be interchangeable:

	  - mediawiki.page.watch.ajax matches .mw-watchlink a[data-mw-interface] to upgrade the
	    watchstar into an AJAX toggle - attribute on the anchor.
	  - mediawiki.misc-authed-curate's rollback.js delegates on
	    .mw-rollback-link a[data-mw-interface] - attribute on the anchor.
	  - the same module's patrol.js matches .patrollink[data-mw-interface] a instead, with
	    the attribute on the CONTAINING element and the anchor as its descendant. It is not
	    "the same way" as rollback, and a change that moved the attribute off a container
	    would break patrolling while leaving rollback working.
	  - mediawiki.page.ready binds the logout link through config.selectorLogoutLink, which
	    is supplied per skin through the SkinPageReadyConfig hook; core's own default in that
	    module's config.json is only a placeholder, so the working selector is whatever the
	    skin sets, not a fixed #pt-logout a[data-mw-interface].
	  - the attribute is not exclusive to chrome anchors either: the same module tests
	    .catlinks[data-mw-interface] and table.diff[data-mw-interface] on content containers.

	Every anchor this partial renders is chrome, so the attribute is emitted unconditionally
	and without a value; where core additionally supplies it through array-attributes - it
	does for the logout and watch links - the repetition is inert, because presence is all
	any of those selectors tests.

	Appearance is owned entirely by the stylesheet. This partial writes no class of its
	own, not even a notion- one, and no inline style: anchors are styled by
	resources/skins.notion.styles/links.less, which applies the Codex link mixins through
	the deliberately low-specificity selector a:where( :not( [role='button'] ) ) so that
	gadgets, user styles and extensions can still override them. The gap between glyph and
	label belongs to components/Icon.less, and that is why the four tags below are joined
	by empty comments: a real newline between them would collapse to a text space inside
	this inline element and add whitespace no stylesheet asked for.

	For the same reason the closing delimiter of this comment is glued to the anchor rather
	than left on a line of its own. Please do not tidy that up. A comment is only stripped
	together with its line when it stands alone on that line of OUTPUT, and this partial is
	rendered mid-line - MenuListItem.mustache invokes it inside a list item - so a newline
	between the delimiter and the anchor is not standalone and survives into the page as a
	stray text node the reference skin does not emit.
}}<a data-mw-interface {{#array-attributes}} {{key}}="{{value}}"{{/array-attributes}}>{{!
	}}{{#icon}}{{>Icon}}{{/icon}}{{!
	}}<span>{{text}}</span>{{!
}}</a>
